Adds the three batch fields to the Edit-slab screen, beside Production Date and
Thickness, each with an "apply to this slab and every following one" toggle.

Unlike Date/Thickness (which live on the slab), Batch No., Design and Target
Slabs live on the shared batch RECIPE, so changing them for a range is not a
field write -- it is a SPLIT. Ticking a field's box moves this slab and every
following slab of the run into the target batch: joined if a batch of that
number+design already exists in the shift, otherwise created from this batch's
own setup (machines, program, thickness, date copied). Earlier slabs stay put;
each slab's own times, delays and per-slab date travel with it. This is how two
runs recorded as one batch -- 1372 Crystallo and 1404 Bellagio green merged --
get separated; a 3-batch mix is two forward-splits.

- batchSplit.ts (pure, tested): resolveTargetBatch (ticked field wins, else the
  current batch's value) and isReparent/sameBatchIdentity (a real move only when
  number or design changed; matched on number AND design, design case-insensitive;
  Target-Slabs-only is a plain attribute edit on the batch, no move).
- production/[id] PATCH: after the date/thickness range step, re-parents the
  forward run (forwardRunIds over the recipe's slabs) to the found-or-created
  target recipe, in the same transaction, and logs it (kind "edit") so the split
  is auditable and reversible. Only runs when a box is ticked and the slab has a
  batch.
- RoboEntryForm: the three fields + toggles in a clearly-marked "split a mixed
  run" block, shown only when editing a slab that is in a batch; sent to the API
  only when a box is ticked, so changing a field alone never moves a slab.

No schema or DB change -- re-parenting uses the existing batchRecipeId; new
recipes are ordinary rows. Verified: tsc clean, 699 tests pass, next build clean.

